comments[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-21T08:27:20.569Z","harness:claude-code","A second remedy-accuracy case, from CodeRabbit on PR #1762: the drift scanner reports history_drift_chain_mismatch both for a bridgeable break and for a patch that only applies on top of the unrecorded change. So when every drifted stream is a chain mismatch, pm health advises pm history-repair --all --append-only, which bridges the first kind and refuses the second with patch_does_not_replay; strict health stays unclean. The fix belongs with this item: the scanner, or the remedy selection, must tell a bridgeable chain mismatch from one that needs the rewriting repair, and the integration spec must run each advised remedy to a clean history_drift check."
  "2026-11-21T11:55:01.454Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation on branch fix/history-remedies-converge, 2026-11-21. (1) Missing stream: history-repair, in both the rewriting and the append-only mode, rebuilds a live item's absent or record-less stream (src/sdk/history/reseed.ts). It writes one sealed history_repair baseline from the empty document to the on-disk item, after any whitespace the stream held, with context.history_reseed {contract_version 1, reason missing_stream|empty_stream, prior_versions unrecorded}, and the result carries a typed reseed receipt. runHistoryMaintenance takes an optional reseed plan, called only for a live item, so a deleted item's empty stream and the evidence-bound modes (merge reconcile audit, receipt proofs, forced audit) keep refusing. The default history.missing_stream auto_create policy recreates a deleted stream as an empty file on read, which is why the reported refusal was 'nothing to repair' rather than 'no stream'. (2) Chain mismatch, from the CodeRabbit comment: pm health now runs the append-only repair's own preview for every chain-mismatched stream (src/sdk/governance/health/history-drift-remedies.ts). A refusal turns the warning into history_drift_chain_unbridgeable with the remedy pm history-repair <id>. When the rewriting preview refuses too, the warning becomes history_drift_chain_unrepairable with the remedy pm history <id> --verify --full plus a version-control restore. details.chain_unbridgeable lists each stream with its reason and rewrite_refusal. (3) Running the remedies surfaced two more cases that never converged. A sealed record edited without a reseal made the rewriting repair throw an untyped TypeError (unknown_error, record_hash_mismatch:entry_1), also for _workspace. It now refuses typed with history_record_integrity_invalid, exit 4, and names the restore. The unreadable-stream remedy named pm history-repair <id>, which the decoder refuses, so it now names the salvage preview (also in validate). (4) Bulk selection: --all --append-only only when every drifted stream is a bridgeable chain mismatch or a missing stream; otherwise --all for the other repair codes, with a bridgeable mismatch keeping its per-item append-only remedy. While any stream is unreadable, unrepairable or version-skewed, every remedy stays per item, because both bulk forms would fail that row. A version-skewed stream is not previewed and keeps only its upgrade remedy, so its redundant chain_mismatch warning is dropped. The new spec tests/integration/lifecycle/history-remedies.integration.spec.ts runs every advised remedy, per item and in bulk, to a clean history_drift check."
  "2026-11-21T12:39:42.209Z","harness:claude-code","Review round 1 on PR #1763 (Codex gpt-6.2-sol xhigh, Greptile, CodeRabbit), 2026-11-21. Fixed: (a) the reseed baseline is appended through O_APPEND to the exact bytes the under-lock check verified, or the stream is created exclusively (wx), in both repair modes. A writer that ignores the lock, or a stale-lock takeover, can therefore no longer lose bytes to a whole-file replace (appendRecordToVerifiedStream in src/sdk/history/append-only-repair.ts). The rewriting repair's own whole-file rewrite stays with pm-r5yhxn. (b) An unrepairable or version-skewed stream keeps only that verdict: its hash- and chain-mismatch warnings no longer advise a repair that refuses, and details keep the evidence. (c) A pending merge-receipt stream blocks the bulk remedies, because the bulk run would reconcile it without the merge audit. (d) A record sealed in a newer record_hash_version format now counts as version skew in the drift scan, and the rewriting repair refuses it typed with history_record_version_unsupported plus the upgrade step, instead of diagnosing corruption. (e) A reseed reports an explicit --normalize-provenance request. Declined with reason: ownership-aware health advice. Health judges the stream, not the caller's authority. Under strict ownership_enforcement the advised repair refuses typed with the --force override and still works for the assignee, and making previews enforce ownership would misclassify an owned stream as unbridgeable."
